### 3.3.2 Exploit Protection (Successor to EMET)

System-wide and **per-application** mitigations, configurable through *Windows Security → App & browser control → Exploit protection*, PowerShell, XML, or Intune/GPO.

| Mitigation | How It Works (Under the Hood) | Notes |
|-----------|-------------------------------|-------|
| **DEP (Data Execution Prevention)** | Uses the CPU **NX/XD bit** in page-table entries to mark data pages (stack, heap) non-executable; executing them raises an access violation. | Policy via `bcdedit /set nx OptIn|OptOut|AlwaysOn|AlwaysOff`. x64 always enforces for 64-bit processes. Defeated by ROP/ret2libc, so it is paired with ASLR/CFG/CET. |
| **ASLR (Address Space Layout Randomization)** | Randomizes base addresses of images, stack, heap, PEB/TEB (and kernel: KASLR). Options: *Force randomization for images (Mandatory ASLR)*, *Randomize memory allocations (bottom-up)*, *High-entropy ASLR* (64-bit, much larger entropy). | Images must be linked with `/DYNAMICBASE`; *Mandatory ASLR* relocates even non-opted-in images. Defeated by information leaks. |
| **CFG (Control Flow Guard)** | Compiler (`/guard:cf`) inserts a check before each *indirect call*; target must be a valid function entry per a **CFG bitmap** maintained by the kernel/loader (`ntdll!LdrpValidateUserCallTarget`). Invalid target → fast-fail (`__fastfail`). | Mitigates vtable/function-pointer hijacking. Successor: **XFG (eXtended Flow Guard)** (type-hash check) and **CET shadow stacks** (see below). |
| **SEHOP (Structured Exception Handler Overwrite Protection)** | Validates that the SEH chain on the stack terminates at `ntdll!FinalExceptionHandler` before dispatching an exception. Overwritten SEH records break the chain. | Only relevant to 32-bit processes (x64 uses table-based exceptions). |
| Hardware-enforced Stack Protection (**CET**) | Shadow stack in hardware stores return addresses; mismatches on `RET` cause a fault. Defeats ROP. | Windows 10 2004+/Win11 on supported CPUs; opt-in per process. |
| **Arbitrary Code Guard (ACG)** | Prevents allocating/modifying executable memory (`VirtualProtect` to RWX, `VirtualAlloc` EXEC) | Breaks JIT-based apps, so use for hardened hosts. |
| **Code Integrity Guard (CIG)** | Process loads only Microsoft/Store-signed DLLs | Used by browser sandboxes. |
| **Block remote images / low-integrity images** | Prevents DLL loads from UNC paths or low-IL locations (DLL planting) | |
| **Block child process creation** | Process cannot spawn child processes | Mitigates RCE-to-shell chains. |
| **Export / Import address filtering (EAF/IAF)** | Guard pages on export/import tables detect shellcode resolving APIs (`kernel32` exports) | Compat issues with some security tools. |
| **Heap termination on corruption** | Terminates process on heap metadata corruption | Enabled by default for modern apps. |
| **Validate heap integrity / handle usage / image dependency signing** | Additional hardening | |
| **Disable Win32k system calls** | Eliminates win32k attack surface for non-GUI processes | |

```powershell
Get-ProcessMitigation -System                       # system defaults
Get-ProcessMitigation -Name winword.exe
Set-ProcessMitigation -Name acrord32.exe -Enable DisallowChildProcessCreation, BlockRemoteImageLoads
Set-ProcessMitigation -System -Enable DEP,SEHOP,ForceRelocateImages
Get-ProcessMitigation -RegistryConfigFilePath settings.xml   # import/export config for GPO (ProcessMitigations XML)
```
Per-app settings are stored under `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\<exe>\MitigationOptions`. Verify with Process Explorer's *Security* tab or `Get-ProcessMitigation`.

### 3.3.3 Network Protection

- Extends **Microsoft Defender SmartScreen** reputation checks to **all outbound HTTP(S) connections from any process** (not just Edge), blocking connections to low-reputation IPs/domains and malicious URLs, plus custom indicators from Defender for Endpoint.
- Hooks in the network stack (WFP) and inspects connection metadata. Modes: Disabled / **Block** / Audit.
```powershell
Set-MpPreference -EnableNetworkProtection Enabled     # or AuditMode
Get-MpPreference | Select EnableNetworkProtection
```
Events: **1125** (audit) / **1126** (block) in `Windows Defender/Operational`; GPO: *Microsoft Defender Exploit Guard → Network Protection*. Windows 11 also offers **Web content filtering** (Defender for Endpoint) and Enhanced Phishing Protection in SmartScreen (warns when typing the Windows password into untrusted apps/sites).

### 3.3.4 Controlled Folder Access (CFA) & Ransomware Mitigation

- CFA allows only **trusted apps** (determined by reputation / Microsoft-signed / allowlist) to write to protected folders (Documents, Pictures, Videos, Music, Favorites, Desktop, plus custom ones).
- Implemented through a Defender minifilter driver (`WdFilter.sys`), which evaluates write operations by the calling process.
```powershell
Set-MpPreference -EnableControlledFolderAccess Enabled          # AuditMode for testing
Add-MpPreference -ControlledFolderAccessProtectedFolders "D:\Finance"
Add-MpPreference -ControlledFolderAccessAllowedApplications "C:\Apps\trusted.exe"
```
Events: **1123** (blocked), **1124** (audited), **5007** (config change).

**Layered ransomware strategy:**

| Layer | Controls |
|-------|----------|
| Prevention | ASR rules (Office children, scripts, email executables, ransomware rule), SmartScreen/Network Protection, patching, MFA, disabling macros from the internet (Mark-of-the-Web) |
| Execution control | WDAC/AppLocker/Smart App Control |
| Containment | CFA, least privilege, LAPS, network segmentation, SMB hardening (disable SMBv1), tamper protection |
| Recovery | **3-2-1 offline/immutable backups**, VSS protection, OneDrive Files Restore / version history |
| Detection | Defender for Endpoint EDR, canary files, honeypot ACL auditing, Event 4663 mass renames |
| Credentials | Credential Guard, LSASS PPL, Protected Users, tiered admin model |

*Tamper Protection* prevents disabling Defender features via registry/PowerShell/GPO (managed via Intune/Security portal), which is a key control because attackers routinely run `Set-MpPreference -DisableRealtimeMonitoring $true`.

