## 3.5 User Account Control (UAC)

UAC enforces least privilege for administrators through **Admin Approval Mode (AAM)**.

### 3.5.1 Split Token Mechanism
When a member of Administrators logs on, LSASS creates **two linked tokens**:
- **Filtered (limited) token:** admin group SIDs set to *deny-only*, high-risk privileges removed (keeps `SeChangeNotify`, `SeShutdown`, `SeUndock`, `SeIncreaseWorkingSet`, `SeTimeZone`), **Medium integrity**. Used for the shell (`explorer.exe`) and all normal processes.
- **Full (elevated) token:** all groups and privileges, **High integrity**. Used only after consent.

### 3.5.2 Elevation Flow
```
User launches app requiring admin (manifest requestedExecutionLevel=requireAdministrator, or "Run as administrator")
 → ShellExecute / CreateProcess fails with ERROR_ELEVATION_REQUIRED
 → Application Information service (AppInfo, svchost) receives the request via RPC
 → AppInfo launches consent.exe (as SYSTEM) 
 → Consent UI shown (on the Secure Desktop if configured)
      • Admin user: "Consent prompt" (Yes/No)
      • Standard user: "Credential prompt" (admin username/password)
 → On approval AppInfo creates the process with the FULL token (CreateProcessAsUser), parent set to the requester
```
- **Secure Desktop:** `consent.exe` runs on a separate, isolated desktop (`Winlogon` desktop) that user-mode processes (even same-user, medium IL) cannot interact with, preventing spoofed clicks, screen capture, or synthetic input to the prompt. The screen dims as the desktop is switched.
- **Manifest execution levels:** `asInvoker`, `highestAvailable`, `requireAdministrator`. Legacy apps without manifests get **installer detection** heuristics (names like `setup.exe`) and **file/registry virtualization** (writes to `Program Files` / `HKLM\Software` redirected to per-user `VirtualStore`).
- **Auto-elevation:** certain signed Microsoft binaries in trusted directories with `autoElevate=true` in the manifest (for example, `fodhelper.exe`, `eventvwr.exe`, `sdclt.exe`) elevate **without a prompt** under the default slider setting. This design is the basis of many **UAC bypasses** (registry hijack of `ms-settings\shell\open\command`, COM hijack, DLL side-loading, token manipulation). Microsoft's servicing policy: **UAC is not a security boundary** (bypasses are defense-in-depth issues, not CVE-class), whereas **admin → kernel/SYSTEM** is also treated as not a boundary. Hence, treat any admin-level process as potentially compromised and use standard-user accounts for daily work.
- **Administrator protection (Windows 11, newer builds):** a newer model where elevation uses a separate, system-managed hidden admin account profile, with Windows Hello authentication and just-in-time token. Treat as an evolving feature and verify availability.

### 3.5.3 Policy Settings (`Security Settings → Local Policies → Security Options` / `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System`)

| Policy | Registry Value | Notes |
|--------|----------------|-------|
| Run all administrators in Admin Approval Mode | `EnableLUA = 1` | Disabling turns UAC (and many Store app features) off, so don't |
| Behavior of elevation prompt for **administrators** | `ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin` | 0 = elevate without prompting; 1 = prompt for credentials on secure desktop; 2 = prompt for consent on secure desktop; **5 = consent for non-Windows binaries (default)**; 3/4 = prompt on normal desktop variants |
| Behavior for **standard users** | `ConsentPromptBehaviorUser` | 0 = auto-deny; 1 = credentials on secure desktop; 3 = credentials (normal desktop) |
| Switch to the secure desktop when prompting | `PromptOnSecureDesktop = 1` | Disable only for remote-assistance scenarios |
| Detect application installations and prompt | `EnableInstallerDetection` | |
| Only elevate executables that are signed and validated | `ValidateAdminCodeSignatures` | PKI enforcement |
| Only elevate UIAccess apps installed in secure locations | `EnableSecureUIAPaths` | |
| Virtualize file and registry write failures | `EnableVirtualization` | |
| Admin Approval Mode for the built-in Administrator | `FilterAdministratorAccount` | Built-in RID-500 account is exempt by default |
| Remote UAC / filtering for network logons | `LocalAccountTokenFilterPolicy` (0 = filter, 1 = full token for local admins over network) | Setting to 1 enables **pass-the-hash for all local admins** (lateral movement) |

**Slider mapping:** *Always notify* (secure desktop, includes Windows setting changes) → *Notify only when apps try to make changes (default)* → *same but without dimming* → *Never notify* (not recommended; with UAC enabled, still isolates tokens but removes prompts).

*Hint: UAC is not the same as "run as admin".* Elevation changes the token, not the user.

