## 3.7 Application Control & Modern Hardening

### 3.7.1 WDAC (App Control for Business) vs. AppLocker

**Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC)**, since rebranded **App Control for Business**, enforces code integrity policies through the **kernel Code Integrity (CI) engine**: it covers drivers, user-mode binaries, DLLs, scripts (via script hosts that call `WldpQuerySecurityPolicy`/`WldpGetLockdownPolicy`, for example PowerShell Constrained Language Mode), MSI, and packaged apps. Policies can be signed; with **HVCI** the policy can be shielded from tampering.

**Policy building blocks:**
- **Rules (levels):** `Hash`, `FileName`, `FilePublisher`, `Publisher`, `PCACertificate`, `SignedVersion`, `FilePath`, `WHQL`, plus **Managed Installer** (trust software deployed by MECM/Intune) and **Intelligent Security Graph (ISG)** (cloud reputation) trust.
- **Policy rule options (selected):** `0` Enabled:UMCI (user-mode enforcement), `3` Audit Mode, `6` Unsigned System Integrity Policy, `9` Advanced Boot Options Menu, `11` Disabled:Script Enforcement, `13` Managed Installer, `14` ISG Authorization, `16` Update Policy No Reboot, `17` Allow Supplemental Policies, `19` Dynamic Code Security.
- **Multiple Policy Format:** *base* policies plus *supplemental* policies (extend allow-lists) and deny policies. Up to 32 active policies.
- **Microsoft recommended block rules** (LOLBins: `msbuild.exe`, `mshta.exe`, `wmic`, etc.) and **driver block rules** can be merged in.

**Workflow:**
```powershell
# 1. Create policy from a reference ("gold") system, audit mode first
New-CIPolicy -Level FilePublisher -Fallback Hash -FilePath .\Base.xml -UserPEs -ScanPath C:\ 
# Or start from a template: C:\Windows\schemas\CodeIntegrity\ExamplePolicies\{AllowMicrosoft,DefaultWindows_Audit,...}.xml
Set-RuleOption -FilePath .\Base.xml -Option 3            # audit mode
ConvertFrom-CIPolicy .\Base.xml .\{PolicyGUID}.cip       # binary policy
# 2. Deploy (Win11 22H2+/Server 2025): 
CiTool.exe --update-policy .\{PolicyGUID}.cip
#    or copy to C:\Windows\System32\CodeIntegrity\CiPolicies\Active\ ; or Intune (OMA-URI / App Control profile) / GPO
# 3. Review events in Microsoft-Windows-CodeIntegrity/Operational (3076 audit, 3077 block) and
#    Microsoft-Windows-AppLocker/MSI and Script (script), then refine → switch to enforced (remove option 3)
```
Tooling: **App Control Wizard** (GUI), `CiTool.exe --list-policies`. Also, **Event 3089** records signing info for events 3076/3077.

**AppLocker (legacy):** user-mode only; policy applied via **GPO/MDM** to a specific user/group SID, evaluated by the **Application Identity service (`AppIDSvc`)** and the `appid.sys` driver; rules are *Allow* (default-deny) plus *Deny* exceptions, in **collections**: Executable (`.exe/.com`), Windows Installer, Script (`.ps1/.vbs/.js/.bat/.cmd`), **DLL** (off by default; performance cost), Packaged apps (MSIX). Rule conditions: **Publisher**, **Path**, **File hash**. Events in `Microsoft-Windows-AppLocker/EXE and DLL` (8002 allowed, 8003 audit-would-block, 8004 blocked) and `MSI and Script` (8005/8006/8007).

| Feature | **WDAC / App Control for Business** | **AppLocker** |
|--------|--------------------------------------|---------------|
| Enforcement layer | **Kernel** Code Integrity (ci.dll) + script hosts | User-mode service/driver (`AppIDSvc`, `appid.sys`) |
| Covers kernel-mode drivers | **Yes** | No |
| Scope | Per **device** (all users) | Per **user/group** possible |
| Tamper resistance | Strong: signed policies, HVCI/secure boot protected, admin cannot easily override | Weaker: local admin can stop `AppIDSvc` / alter policy (unless protected by GPO + other controls) |
| Rule types | Signer, hash, filename, path (runtime/user-writable checks), ISG, Managed Installer | Publisher, path, hash |
| Reputation (ISG) / Managed Installer | **Yes** | No |
| Multiple policies / supplemental | **Yes** | No (single rule set; GPO merge) |
| Audit mode | Yes (`3076`/`3077`) | Yes (`8003`/`8006`) |
| Management | Intune, MECM, GPO (single policy), PowerShell, `CiTool`, Wizard | GPO, Intune (CSP), PowerShell |
| Windows SKU | All Windows 10/11 (authoring in Pro; some GPO deployment Enterprise/Education) | Enforcement requires Enterprise/Education (Pro can't enforce via GPO in older versions) |
| Microsoft guidance | **Security feature**: recommended for new deployments | Defense-in-depth; **no new features**, still supported |
| Bypass risks | Misconfigured allow-rules (writable paths, signed LOLBins, trusted Managed Installer abuse), unsigned policy tamper if no secure boot | User-writable allowed paths (for example `C:\Windows\Tasks`, `Temp`), LOLBins, running as admin, DLL rules off |

Recommended practice: start with **audit mode**, deploy a **base + supplemental** structure, block known bypass binaries, use **signed policies** (and UEFI-lock where fitting), and keep AppLocker only where per-user rules are needed (both can coexist; WDAC wins when both apply, because they are layered and both must allow).

### 3.7.2 Smart App Control (SAC)

**Smart App Control** (Windows 11 22H2+) is a **consumer-oriented, prebuilt App Control policy** that blocks untrusted/unknown apps by default. It is built on WDAC and the **Intelligent Security Graph (ISG)**.

**How it decides to run a binary:**
```
Launch → CI engine intercepts image load
  1. Is the file signed by a certificate/signer in Microsoft's trusted signer set (valid, not abusive)?  → allow
  2. Else ask Microsoft's cloud intelligence service (ML/"AI-driven" predictions plus reputation) → "known good" → allow
  3. Else unsigned/unknown → block (user sees "Smart App Control blocked part of this app")
Also blocks: apps known to be malicious/PUA, certain script hosts/macros-based abuse paths per policy,
 and abuse-prone LOLBin configurations; applies to executables, DLLs, scripts, and installers.
```
- **States:** *Evaluation mode* (learns whether it will be usable for your workflow, auto-turns Off if disruptive) → **On** (enforced) or **Off**. Historically, requires a **clean install** to enter Evaluation; once turned **Off**, re-enabling traditionally required a Windows reset/reinstall (Microsoft has been changing this, so verify the current build behavior). Not available when an enterprise WDAC policy is managing the device (SAC turns off).
- **Status check:** *Windows Security → App & browser control → Smart App Control settings*, or the registry `HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\CI\Policy` value **`VerifiedAndReputablePolicyState`** (`0` = Off, `1` = On/enforce, `2` = Evaluation).
- **Pros:** blocks unsigned commodity malware and trojanized installers *without* admin policy engineering; works with Microsoft Defender SmartScreen (Mark-of-the-Web based reputation) and Defender.
- **Cons/trade-offs:** blocks legitimate unsigned developer tools/internal apps (developers should use Dev Drive/signed builds or turn off), requires cloud connectivity for reputation lookups, not manageable at fine granularity, and signed-but-abusable binaries can still be misused (hence layering with ASR/EDR).
- **Enterprise stance:** use **WDAC/App Control for Business** (centrally managed, auditable) instead of SAC, and use SmartScreen + Defender protections.

